You don't mention what version of Kermit you are using, or much about your
set-up.

I don't think that there is such a thing as VAX Kermit.  There are only 
MS-DOS Kermit and C-Kermit.  Both are available at kermit.columbia.edu.

MS-Kermit is at version 3.13.  C-Kermit is at about version 5A(190) or so.
You have to get the C code for C-Kermit and then compile it on your machine.

Instead of typing all the settings I use each time, put them all in an
initialization file and then tell Kermit to use that initalization file.
These files are automatically read by Kermit every time you start it up.
For MS-Kermit, the file is MSCUSTOM.INI
For C-Kermit, the file will be ckermit.ini for VMS, or .kermrc for unix,
or some logical variant of these.

Some of the commands I have found useful to put in these files include:

SET LINE
SET BLOCK-CHECK 3
SET WINDOWS 4
SET SEND PACKET 2000
SET RECEIVE PACKET 2000
SET FILE TYPE TEXT
SET FILE COLLISION RENAME


I believe that both MS-Kermit and C-Kermit both have context sensitive
help.  That is, if you type: set ?   It will display what kinds of things
you can set; and typing: set file ?  will show what appropriate things you
can set for the file parameter.

I find it most useful to use the SERVER command in Kerit.  That way you
know that your connection is established.

Try connecting to your host, start Kermit, set up the paramaters you want
(ie: set file type text) then type server, go back to your local Kermit
and then transfer files using GET and PUT.  Then type FIN or FINISH, when
you are through and then C to connect back up to your server.

With server mode, you don't have to worry about getting back to your local
Kermit and typing a send or receive command before Kermit at the other
end times-out and gives up.

Also,if your system is set up with TCP/IP software, you can connect to any
host kinda like TELNET via the command: set host foo.bar.com

Of course, you might have to upgrade whatever versions of Kermit your
machines are running to use these features.


--Jason Hunsaker
